                "entry_id": "4dd0e6cb-689b-3bbb-a24b-ac2c9854958c",
                "word": "scutoïde",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "6be9b36e-5b96-4872-9026-7375514d5973",
                        "entry_id": "4dd0e6cb-689b-3bbb-a24b-ac2c9854958c",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "scutoïdes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4a01e5fa-0299-3b49-ae04-6df7950e7b4c",
                        "entry_id": "4dd0e6cb-689b-3bbb-a24b-ac2c9854958c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Solide géométrique entre deux surfaces parallèles."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5bb4c0cc-194d-4f87-a92e-50a87d4a3347",
                                "sense_id": "4a01e5fa-0299-3b49-ae04-6df7950e7b4c",
                                "text": "Ressemblant à un prisme un peu tordu, le scutoïde a été découvert par des chercheurs espagnols et américains dans les cellules épithéliales (qui recouvrent la surface externe ou interne des organes) où il favoriserait la stabilité lors des torsions de tissus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b8447fbe-7974-4fd4-8bef-9fddde870d55",
                                "sense_id": "4a01e5fa-0299-3b49-ae04-6df7950e7b4c",
                                "text": "Comme cette structure n’avait pas de nom mathématique, elle a été appelée « scutoïde », en référence au scutellum des insectes."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "04499d97-04d7-45d0-a477-b79eae5233a5",
                        "source_entry_id": "4dd0e6cb-689b-3bbb-a24b-ac2c9854958c",
                        "source_sense_id": "4a01e5fa-0299-3b49-ae04-6df7950e7b4c",
                        "target_lang_word": "Scutoid",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7dcfa334-6e62-4bd3-a3b1-924db1057055",
                        "source_entry_id": "4dd0e6cb-689b-3bbb-a24b-ac2c9854958c",
                        "source_sense_id": "4a01e5fa-0299-3b49-ae04-6df7950e7b4c",
                        "target_lang_word": "scutoid",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "scutoid",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3ea9e6ed-cec9-34bc-8a7c-b7794dd44182",
                                        "entry_id": "0d063f78-f0f2-363e-8652-c997abf1b760",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A geometrical solid described as a mix between a frustum and a prismatoid."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1c5f1c4d-cafd-4857-941a-0620c0a7be98",
                                                "sense_id": "3ea9e6ed-cec9-34bc-8a7c-b7794dd44182",
                                                "text": "A scutoid can be regarded as a prism that is truncated at one or more vertices, with faces and edges sized such that suitably oriented copies of said scutoid can be arranged to fully pack the space between two planes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "690cdab6-eef8-462f-acc7-dde6e25f2fb4",
                                                "sense_id": "3ea9e6ed-cec9-34bc-8a7c-b7794dd44182",
                                                "text": "As a consequence, epithelial cells adopt a novel shape that we term “scutoid”.[…]Using biophysical arguments, we propose that scutoids make possible the minimization of the tissue energy and stabilize three-dimensional packing."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d24d09be-0c0f-4428-982a-6a73e0af4fa8",
                                                "sense_id": "3ea9e6ed-cec9-34bc-8a7c-b7794dd44182",
                                                "text": "Our energetics analysis reveals that scutoids allow tissues to minimize the packing energy and we propose that such geometrical shape is nature’s solution to epithelial bending."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6e5a0c0b-75a1-44bf-b7f0-0c8774a8b9e1",
                                                "sense_id": "3ea9e6ed-cec9-34bc-8a7c-b7794dd44182",
                                                "text": "It has been postulated that ‘scutoids’ are part of a continuum of cell shapes and 3D arrangements, together with frusta and prisms"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "scutoid",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "17cd90f1-3ff9-3c2f-afb2-3e0c6a6dd4aa",
                                        "entry_id": "02502ba4-8eb5-3ec5-8140-72bd01eaa7bf",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative letter-case form of Scutoid, a genetic mutation in Drosophila melanogaster"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
